{
  "requirement": "Every {term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain` implements\n`habeas_sdk::domain::Domain`. A domain is cloneable and safe to share between\nexecutions. The following chapters introduce its members one capability at a\ntime:\n\n```rust,ignore\n// Defined in habeas_sdk::domain.\n/// Application data and operations used to evaluate criteria.\npub trait Domain: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ... shared error type introduced below ...\n    // ... node identities and contents in Graph nodes ...\n    // ... labeled relationships in Graph edges ...\n    // ... evidence persistence in Storage ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judged this from the supplied test sources only and requested no implementation source. The requirement `requirements.sdk.domain` has three testable obligations. Every application domain implements `habeas_sdk::domain::Domain`. `Domain` is `Clone + Send + Sync`. A domain is safe to share between executions. Its member chapters are covered by the other requirements the tests also cite, so I did not assess them here.\n\n- **Trait contract and bounds.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]` implements `Domain` for an opaque `Relationships` fixture. Its generic `check_bounds<G: Domain>()` calls `domain::<G>()`, which requires `Clone + Send + Sync`. Those bounds are therefore enforced at compile time, and loosening any of them would break the build. The same test checks the other associated types. It then calls the trait's operations through the trait: `node_content`, `load_evidence`, `store_evidence`, `node_path`, and `outgoing_edges`. This shows the fixture works as a `Domain`.\n- **Cloneable.** Many tests clone the domain into a validator or graph. Examples are `Validator::new(domain.clone())` in `contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` and in `agent.rs#fn[behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer]`. Those tests then assert on state seen through the original handle. For example, the domain's record count and `reads` are checked after the validator runs. This shows clones share state.\n- **Safe to share between executions.**\n  - `contracts.rs#fn[concurrent_same_criterion_executions_keep_complete_independent_records]` runs two executions on one domain behind a barrier and a timeout. It asserts that both run concurrently and that their records are independent.\n  - `validation.rs#fn[concurrent_single_node_validations_keep_separate_traces]` runs three concurrent validations and asserts that traces stay separate.\n  - `validation.rs#fn[shared_context_allows_independent_concurrent_agent_calls]` and `agent.rs#fn[concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads]` share one domain across concurrent agent calls and assert the outcomes.\n- **Application domains used with the SDK.** Tests implement real domains (`Memory`, `Application`) and run the SDK against them end to end. `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[domain_formats_supplied_contents_without_resolving_a_replacement]` covers the Habeas domain's content formatting.\n\nI found no plausible violation of the subject's obligations that these tests would accept. A domain that is not `Clone`, `Send`, or `Sync` would fail to compile in `check_bounds`. A domain that could not be shared between concurrent executions would fail the concurrency tests. No gaps; no acknowledgments are needed.",
  "references": "ed9ccc46ca7c14b9cdfc7ded9a2564da2295f3d6c8694a892bc5e88cc71c5a03",
